    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d164">

                <pb facs="0355" n="341" sameAs="frus:frus1944v01/pg341"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 E.A.C./9–2144: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Winant</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-09-21T23:00:00+01:00">September 21,
                            1944—11 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received September 21—8:23 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>7856. Comea 93. My 7679, September 17, 9 p.m. Today Strang delivered to me and
                    the Soviet Ambassador letters withdrawing the reservations attached in his
                    August 31 letters<anchor n="85" xml:id="d164-f1"/> to the United Kingdom
                    approval of the German surrender instrument. Text follows by air.<anchor n="86"
                        xml:id="d164-f2"/> I hope that the Soviet Government will now withdraw its
                    reservation set forth in Gousev’s letter of September 14.<anchor n="87"
                        xml:id="d164-f3"/> If that is done, I believe that we shall have a much more
                    solid tripartite basis of policy in Germany than if the reservations had been
                    allowed to stand.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Winant</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p>[As an enclosure to instruction 4583, September 27, 1944, the Department
                        transmitted to the Ambassador in the United Kingdom for his information and
                        guidance a “Directive to SCAEF [Supreme Commander, Allied Expeditionary
                        Force] Regarding the Military Government of Germany in the Period
                        Immediately Following the Cessation of Organized Resistance (Post-Defeat)”,
                        dated September 22. This directive was prepared by the War and State
                        Departments and concurred in by the Treasury Department. It was included as
                        Enclosure C of Joint Chiefs of Staff document J.C.S. 1067, “Directive for
                        Administration of Post-Defeat Military Government in Germany”, dated
                        September 24, 1944. For texts of the instruction and directive, see <ref
                            target="frus:frus1945Malta/pg_142">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, The Conferences at Malta
                            and Yalta, 1945, pages 142</ref>, <ref
                            target="frus:frus1945Malta/pg_143">143</ref>.]</p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
